increasing work capacity is one of the  most effective ways to increase size and  strength and one of the most  underutilized in fact focusing on work  capacity can lead to some truly  superhuman feats and you'll see what I  mean  later on in this video simply put work  capacity describes your ability to train  for an extended period of time the  greater your work capacity the longer  you can train before fitness becomes a  limiting factor if you've ever felt like  you could have done more push-ups or  more pull-ups if you weren't out breath  then work capacity is probably something  that you need to focus on this is  actually one of the main focuses of  CrossFit by the way which is why they're  able to do things like tens or even  hundreds of clean and presses without  tiring out but essentially seeing as  work capacity allows you to train for  longer and harder that means you can  train more and it means you can get more  results in that way it is a kind of  secret sauce when it comes to strength  and conditioning train more get more  results it's that simple if you want to  increase your work capacity what do you  need to do well obviously starting with  a basic level of fitness is rather a  good idea that means you need to have a  decent vo2 max it means you need to  better control your breathing whilst  you're lifting so you're not getting out  of breath however that is just one small  part of it it's not even the main part  that's because work capacity is actually  much more specific than that work  capacity means being able to do more of  the thing that you want to be able to do  lots of and learning to run long  distances doesn't necessarily guarantee  that you're gonna be able to do massive  rep ranges when it comes to your squats  so you're dead lit these are different  muscles it uses different energy systems  there's just only a certain amount of  crossover for example when lifting  weights you really if ever use your  aerobic energy system then other aspects  are more involved in what capacity when  it comes to curling weight such as your  muscular endurance which is the ability  of your muscles to exert force of a  moderate amount over an extended period  of time this has to do with things like  glasses in stores and blood supply and  all kinds of other stuff  and of course there's a danger to adding  a lot of cardio into a strength-based  training routine if you add your cardio  the end of your workouts then you risk  creating a kind of catabolic cocktail of  hormones that might impair your muscle  growth but if you do your cardio at the  beginning of the workout then you'll be  out of breath and you'll be more like to  injure yourself and less likely to have  an effective workout in general so what  they do instead you simply do more of  the thing that you want to do in this  case that means increasing the volume  and the intensity of your workouts  gradually over time thereby testing your  ability to keep going without wearing  out Ross ethically author of the world's  fittest book which is an excellent book  by the way I recommend you check it out  recommend something that he calls horse  power programming this involves a couple  of different strategies for increasing  your work capacity the first of which is  simply to add an extra set onto each  exercise you do this by performing an  extra rep at the end on its own then  increasing those reps one by one each  time you do a workout until eventually  you're doing a whole extra set and then  once you're doing four sets or however  many you can increase the weight and go  back to three and then build up to doing  poor again this works because you're  basically just increasing the amount of  volume thereby increasing your work  capacity however this isn't my favorite  strategy for a couple of reasons the  first is that the length of your  workouts is gonna be very variable now  if you're doing three sets of everything  or four sets of everything then that's  going to drastically increase the amount  of time that you're in the gym and you  might not have that much time it also  increases the risk of injury because of  course you're lifting more weight at a  point of exhaustion and then there's the  whole kettle of fish that is over  training or over reaching there's a  whole video on that and I recommend you  watch that because actually has a lots  to do with this one this is why I prefer  Ross's alternative recommendation which  is to do a kind of finisher at the end  of your workouts and this isn't just a  pure cardiac finisher it's a resistance  cardio finisher that means that you're  combining cardio with some form of  resistance like a weight or like a  pulley or something like that or your  body weight and for bonus points try  make sure that the finisher targets the  same muscles as the workout that you're  doing so an example of this might be to  perform a shoulder workout let's do ten  intervals of two minutes on the battle  ropes you should give this a try it  really pains their shoulders that's  because you're sending tons of blood to  the area you've just trained thereby  causing occlusion  and the build-up of metabolites  basically you're causing a lot of  metabolic stress after you've just done  a whole workout on the area this can  potentially increase your growth as well  as improving your work capacity by  forcing you to keep on going at the  point where you would normally go home  and call it a day there's one more  technique that you can use that's even  more interesting and it's one that ross  also recommends but in a different  context basically you're just going to  add more exercise targeting the same  muscle group at the end of each workout  but this is going to be with a light  weight is going to be a simple movement  and it's going to be non eccentric  concentric only so by simple movement we  mean it's gonna be single joint like a  curl or a common movement pattern like  running or pulling it needs to be low  weight or non-load-bearing or low impact  and finally it needs to be concentric  only concentric only means that you're  only contracting the muscles concentric  part of the movement is where you  shorten the muscle and contract it the  eccentric part is where you lengthen the  muscle normally the eccentric part is  the part where you put the weight back  down an amortization is the part in  between where you switch from concentric  to eccentric there's a new word for you  potentially the eccentric portion of the  movement as most of you will know is  incredibly important because by  lengthening the muscle you can actually  bear more weight than you can when you  shorten it you can lower more weight  than you can lift at the same time this  is when a lot of the muscle damage  occurs to lots of the micro-tears and  that's what leads to strength gains and  increases in size however the eccentric  portion is also the part of the exercise  that causes the Dom's  delayed onset muscle soreness also known  as ouchies the next day at the same time  that's probably what causes the most  information and it can lead to  overtraining and injury so basically by  eliminating that parts of the exercise  and just focusing on concentric we were  able to get some of the benefits still  but at the same time we're avoiding that  Dom's and that pain so in theory after  doing a whole pull workout you could go  and flip tires that's concentric only  exercise because you're just pushing  you're not lowering the tire and you  wouldn't have any problem the next day  and you could do this for large reps at  a high intensity and high volume to  increase your work capacity without the  downsides Ross Edgar Lee used this same  technique in order to achieve his  world's highest rope climb which was the  length of Everest I believe he was able  to train his biceps every  all day using that technique as well as  climbing up a rope and then just walking  down step allows us to get back 